<article class="env-lemma" id="03FZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03FZ">Lemma <span data-tag="03FZ">66.6.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$. Let $U$ be a separated scheme and $U \to X$ étale. Then $U \to X$ is separated, and $R = U \times _ X U$ is a separated scheme. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $X' \subset X$ be the open subscheme such that $U \to X$ factors through an étale surjection $U \to X'$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/06NF" data-tag="06NF">66.4.10</a>. If $U \to X'$ is separated, then so is $U \to X$, see Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02WK" data-tag="02WK">65.5.4</a> (as the open immersion $X' \to X$ is separated by Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02YO" data-tag="02YO">65.5.8</a> and Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01L7" data-tag="01L7">26.23.8</a>). Moreover, since $U \times _{X'} U = U \times _ X U$ it suffices to prove the result after replacing $X$ by $X'$, i.e., we may assume $U \to X$ surjective. Consider the commutative diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ R = U \times _ X U \ar[r] \ar[d] &  U \ar[d] \\  U \ar[r] &  X }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       In the proof of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02X4" data-tag="02X4">65.13.1</a> we have seen that $j : R \to U \times _ S U$ is separated. The morphism of schemes $U \to S$ is separated as $U$ is a separated scheme, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KV" data-tag="01KV">26.21.13</a>. Hence $U \times _ S U \to U$ is separated as a base change, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KU" data-tag="01KU">26.21.12</a>. Hence the scheme $U \times _ S U$ is separated (by the same lemma). Since $j$ is separated we see in the same way that $R$ is separated. Hence $R \to U$ is a separated morphism (by Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KV" data-tag="01KV">26.21.13</a> again). Thus by Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02WZ" data-tag="02WZ">65.11.4</a> and the diagram above we conclude that $U \to X$ is separated. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>